  "_note": "Superset of docks-3d-static: every Docks piece plus the rest of Knights (church, castle, all house variants, statues, paths, canals) and the ruin/camp/rock/weapon cut of Dungeon Realms. One job so static/manifest.json stays one ledger. Left out on purpose: skinned prefabs (the Dungeon Realms tent cloths and flag lines hung the mesh-only static path, 2026-09-11) and everything that is not this world -- gems, gold hoards, dwarf corpses and statues, desert sand, dragon ribs, glowing obelisks, sand skiffs, yurts, tourney tents, the guillotine (Granadad hangs), the crossed church tower and gravestone, the flower, the orange Knights water tile, the steel oil drum (critic pass, 2026-09-11). Still in but read carefully: the Dungeon Realms ruins/rocks are sandstone-and-terracotta (crypts and undercrofts, not the grey street); SM_Gen_Env_Water_Plane_01 exports as grey concrete -- SM_Generic_Water_Plane_01 (Dungeon Realms) reads as water. Second pass after the critic (2026-09-11): PolygonNature rocks, stone walls, broken pillars, dead/swamp trees, ferns, reeds, pebbles and river planes (the wet grey rock the street wants), and from Dungeon Realms the beds, stool, quay crane, shrine, well, fireplace and balustrades. SM_Prop_Plinth_01 cut (carved crosses); Plinth_02 is the plain one. Every PolygonNature piece whose colour lives in its shader graph (trees, stumps, twigs, vines, ferns, grasses, reeds, river planes: a white or noise map under a tint the flattener cannot read) is cut; what stays of Nature is the atlas-textured set (rocks, boulders, rock walls, piles, bricks, fences, broken pillars, stone walls, mushrooms, lily pads, swamp growth, pebbles, mounds, riverside). SM_Env_Shrine_01 cut (a pagoda). Palette warning: the Nature rocks are warm tan (about 145,132,109), lighter than the Dungeon Realms sandstone and nowhere near the Knights masonry (about 81,78,76); SM_Rock_CaveInterior_01/02 are inside-out shells, interior use only. SM_Tree_Birch_Dead_01 (noise-map leaves) and SM_Prop_Grave_03 (a wooden cross) cut on the critic's last pass."
}